{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/1468882-hypothetical-grand-campaign-throughout-the-settings-timeline","id":1468882,"title":"Hypothetical grand campaign throughout the settings timeline","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-11-25T18:58:29-08:00","id":1468882,"title":"Hypothetical grand campaign throughout the settings timeline","content":"This is an idea I'm really looking forward to doing when all the original era books are out. How would you guys go about doing it? Have the players play a separate group of characters in each era, and have their actions in the previous era have an effect of the upcoming eras? It might be interesting to see how some minor action in the Adventure! era might result in 9 still being an agency with the United States government. Maybe have the characters play legacy characters, with them playing actual descendants of their previous player characters or perhaps students or people who've read the diaries of previous player characters?<br /> <br /> Also, would it be cooler to have each era have its own separate storyline and antagonist, or would it be better to make up an antagonist that could go throughout the entire history of the setting? A rogue alien with delusions of godhood, perhaps? A secret society?<br /> <br /> It would be cool if the players completely mess up history. Adventure! leads to a post-apocalyptic modern day setting where Talents rule over the blasted nuclear remnants of Humanity. The Nova age is a time of great rebuilding that ends in a bio weapon being released that removes most of Humanities ability to become Nova's, kills 20 percent of the population and turns another 10 percent into Superiors. Aeon is a time of great prosperity because the infrastructure the Nova's built up didn't get destroyed in a war, so Earth is a united Utopia. Psion's have just showed up relatively recently but they aren't the saviors of mankind, so there is a bit more suspicion about them. It would be cool to see how different things could go.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27133-planguy","name":"Planguy","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-25T19:37:51-08:00","id":1468885,"title":"","content":"Well, that is the fun part. Changing history is not so &quot;renegade&quot; and &quot;forbidden&quot; as you may think.<br /> The games have a very central concept to it which is, it is a multiverse. Everything you do is canon. Each game is meant to be a bubble. Adventure! is it's own thing. Aberrant is it's own thing. So forth and so on. You can include or exclude whatever you want.<br /> Adventure! can be played as the only &quot;super powered&quot; people in the setting ever.<br /> If the actions of the players make it where WW 2 ends with a peaceful surrender. Or that their actions prevent the Cold War, then that is valid and fine and even encouraged.<br /> <br /> If you want to stick to the timeline, then you can do so. Personally, I would go backwards. You have some master villain in Aeon and then, in each setting, you see how things led to them being that way. For example, if the main villain is a powerful Aberrant that is secretly in charge of a corporation, then you can reveal in Anima how they created the corporation. In Aberrant, you could show them how they slipped slowly into being an Aberrant from a normal nova. And in Adventure!, you could have a villain be a cult that foretells of the rise of the Aberrant. And even in Aether, you could probably show someone who is obsessed with finding some artifacts that will reveal &quot;the truth&quot; which is prophecy that the cult from Adventure! clings to.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/786-thedonnie","name":"thedonnie","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-25T19:53:03-08:00","id":1468887,"title":"","content":"Oh, I'm not trying to be a &quot;rebel&quot;, I'm just taking advantage of the setting material and talking about hypothetical game scenarios where players would play through each era in turn but have their previous actions actually change things in a realistic fashion. Like what happens if someone plays Adventure! but in the course of playing through that era the Aeon Society is destroyed? What's the modern setting going to be like? There might have to be some bending of the plot to keep each era's player faction relevant, but that would be part of the fun. I know that a part of the setting is that your actions matter, but how does that look in practice? Like maybe the Nova age feature 100 Nova's being created in a small suburb of Tokyo rather then thousands of Nova's showing up all over the world.<br /> <br /> Something that might be cool is including time travel. Maybe have the protagonists being normal people from Aeon who get blasted back to the Adventure! era and get empowered there. They have imperfect knowledge about history that a person in the Aeon era might learn in high school and know how to use but not create tech from their own time period. Then have the protagonists of the future eras be the people who gain access to the Adventure! era time travelers personal notes. Effectively the heroes of the game's story are a secret society that is trying to create a better future.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27133-planguy","name":"Planguy","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-29T06:22:07-08:00","id":1469168,"title":"","content":"I've been planning a Crisis on Infinite Continuums since the first kickstarter. I've only run the Aeon portion of it yet, but it ended with the Doyen causing a battle between Chromatics, psions, and novas in the space above Eden while using a Doyen-possessed-QK to flood the area with energy. This tore open a hole in the Continuum, and some of the Doyen escaped into it. I'll probably run Aberrant next and have something thematically similar happen at the end. Once I've run a game in each era, I'll run a mash-up game in an overlapping Continuum, where the players will be attempting to save the Continuum.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8719-florin","name":"Florin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"940"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}